  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "An abandoned lifeboat appeared on the world's most remote island. Bouvet. Rock and ice, 1,700 kilometres from Antarctica, never inhabited.",
        "visual_query": "remote antarctic island coast",
        "visual_fallback": "iceberg ocean"
      },
      {
        "narration": "1964. A crew off HMS Protector lands by helicopter at Nyroysa, the only place you can land at all. The boat sits in a lagoon.",
        "visual_query": "royal navy ship antarctic",
        "visual_fallback": "naval vessel"
      },
      {
        "narration": "They search the beach. No people. No footprints. No camp. No other sign that anyone had ever been there. Then, short on time, they fly out and leave it.",
        "visual_query": "black volcanic beach glacier",
        "visual_fallback": "glacier beach"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Fourteen years pass before anyone collects it. Still no bodies. Still nothing naming it. So who reached the loneliest island on Earth?",
        "visual_query": "abandoned wooden boat shore",
        "visual_fallback": "wrecked boat"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The leading answer is Soviet. A whaling fleet vessel reportedly stranded sailors here in 1958. It has never been confirmed \u2014 and no one was ever found.",
        "visual_query": "antarctic whaling ship ice",
        "visual_fallback": "ship in ice"
      }
    ],
